course: everyday
lesson: 328
title: Rackets, running with things in your mouth, football or soccer, offside, goals, sledding, practice and shared gear
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many sports injuries are easy to avoid: keep a safe distance from someone swinging a tennis racket, and never run with a lollipop or pencil in your mouth or something sharp in your hand.
explain: The word «football» means different games: soccer in the UK, Ireland and most of the world, American football in the USA and Canada, and often Australian rules or rugby league in Australia.
explain: In soccer the offside rule says an attacker must not be behind the last defender when the ball is passed forward to them.
explain: Example: a girl in Colorado goes sledding (sledging in the UK) feet first, sitting up, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds, and she stops when it gets dark.
explain: Getting better takes practice: when you get frustrated, break the skill into small steps; if you will miss practice, tell the coach in advance; and clean shared helmets and gloves.
fact: Keep a safe distance from anyone swinging a racket in tennis or badminton, because rackets can hit faces.
fact: Never run with a lollipop or pencil in your mouth or sharp things in your hand; a fall can push it into your throat or eye.
fact: «Football» means soccer in the UK, Ireland and most of the world, but American football in the USA and Canada, and often Australian rules or rugby league in Australia, where people call it «footy».
fact: In soccer, an attacker is offside if they are behind the last defender (nearer the other team's goal than every defender except the goalkeeper) when the ball is passed forward to them.
fact: Never climb on or hang from a soccer goal; unanchored goals can fall and crush children, so tell an adult if one wobbles.
fact: Sled feet first, sitting up, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds; head-first sledding causes injuries.
fact: Stop sledding when it gets dark, because you cannot see obstacles.
fact: When you get frustrated in practice, break the skill into small steps; small steps lead to big improvement.
fact: If you will miss practice, tell the coach in advance, because the team plans around you.
fact: Clean shared helmets and gloves, because shared gear can spread germs and head lice (tiny insects in hair; their eggs are called nits).
exercise: Your friend is swinging a tennis racket to warm up. Where should you stand? | Well out of reach of the racket | Right behind her to watch her grip | Close in front so she can see you | Next to her elbow to help her aim
exercise: Why keep a safe distance from someone swinging a racket? | Rackets can hit faces | Rackets give off a strong wind | The ball might burst near you | It distracts the umpire
exercise: A boy wants to run in the playground with a lollipop in his mouth. What should he do? | Stand still or sit while he eats it | Run but hold the stick tightly with his teeth | Run only on the soft grass | Run slowly with his mouth shut
exercise: Why is running with a pencil in your hand or mouth dangerous? | A fall can push it into your throat or eye | Pencils snap in half when you run and the lead gets everywhere | Running makes the pencil lead poisonous | You may lose the pencil
exercise: Friends from London, Texas and Sydney all say they love «football». What is true? | They may each mean a different game | They all mean exactly the same game | Only the friend from London is right | Football is only played in the USA
exercise: What does «football» usually mean in the USA and Canada? | American football | Soccer | Rugby union | Australian rules
exercise: In soccer, when is an attacker offside? | When behind the last defender as the ball is passed forward | When standing anywhere in their own half of the pitch at the kick-off | When the ball is passed backwards to them | Whenever they touch the ball with their head
exercise: Children are hanging from the crossbar of a soccer goal. What should you do? | Get them to stop and tell an adult if it wobbles | Join in, goals are built strong enough for children to climb on | Hang on the other side to balance it | Push the goal to test how strong it is
exercise: Why should you never climb on a soccer goal? | An unanchored goal can fall and crush children | The net is made of sharp wire and cuts your hands badly | The goal gets dirty and must be washed | Climbing makes the goal shrink
exercise: How should you go sledding (sledging) down a snowy hill? | Feet first, sitting up, away from roads, trees and ponds | Head first on your tummy for more speed and better steering | Standing up on the sled like a surfboard | Towards the frozen pond for a long slide
exercise: Why should you stop sledding when it gets dark? | You cannot see obstacles | Snow melts as soon as the sun sets | Sleds do not slide at night | The hill closes at night by law
exercise: You keep missing a hard skill at football practice and feel frustrated. What helps most? | Break it into small steps | Give up the sport for good | Practise only the parts you already know | Shout at the coach
exercise: Why does breaking a skill into small steps help? | Small steps lead to big improvement | It makes practice finish sooner | It means you never make mistakes | The coach then does it for you
exercise: You have a dentist appointment during swimming practice. What should you do? | Tell the coach in advance | Just not turn up | Tell a teammate a week later | Send a friend in your place
exercise: Why tell the coach in advance when you will miss practice? | The team plans around you | The coach must refund the money | It means you can skip the next one too | Coaches like to be busy
exercise: Your team shares batting helmets. What is a good habit? | Clean shared helmets and gloves | Never wear a helmet that is not yours | Wear two helmets at once | Leave helmets in the sun to dry for a week
test: In badminton at school, a girl walks behind a boy who is about to swing. What should she do? | Wait until he finishes and walk around at a distance | Duck quickly under the racket | Walk past fast behind him | Tap him on the shoulder just as he swings, so he knows she's there
test: Which is the most dangerous thing to do while running? | Holding scissors | Holding a soft ball | Wearing a cap | Carrying a water bottle in a bag
test: A boy in Melbourne says «Let's watch the footy». What might he mean? | Australian rules football | Basketball | American baseball on the big screen | Cricket
test: A striker in soccer is standing behind all the defenders when her teammate passes the ball forward to her. What does the referee call? | Offside | A penalty | A goal kick | A throw-in
test: At a park a soccer goal rocks when the wind blows. What should children do? | Keep off it and tell an adult | Sit on the crossbar to hold it down | Hang on the net to steady it | Kick balls at it to test it
test: Which sledding plan is safest? | Feet first, sitting up, on a hill away from a road | Head first down a long hill that ends at a frozen pond | Feet first, sitting up, at night by the road | Lying down between the trees
test: A girl cannot do a cartwheel and wants to give up. What advice is best? | Practise one small part at a time | Try it once more and then stop forever | Only practise when people are watching | Wait until she is older without practising
test: Which is true about shared sports gear? | Shared helmets and gloves can spread germs and head lice | Sports gear cannot carry germs, because the sweat dries off quickly | Only shoes can spread anything | Cleaning gear makes it weaker
test: Why does a coach want to know beforehand that a player will be away? | To plan the team around who is there | To give the player extra homework | To tell the other players off | To choose a new captain every time
explain: A girl in Colorado sledded head first towards a tree; she could not steer away in time and broke her arm and hit her head.
explain: A boy in England stood close to a friend practising tennis serves; the racket hit him in the face and broke his nose.
fact: Sledding head first makes head and neck injuries more likely.
fact: Rackets and bats swung near people cause many face and eye injuries.
test: sled head first down the big hill, faster! | Sit up feet first, away from trees and roads | Head first is the most fun and you go loads faster | Head first if you wear a hat | Head first if the hill is short
test: getting dark, one more run on the sled? | Stop now; you can't see obstacles | One more is fine | Go faster to finish | Do it with your eyes half closed
test: standing right behind my mate while he serves | Stand well back from the racket | Stand close to watch | Stand right in front | Stand on his side
test: did u hear a kid ran with a lollipop in his mouth, tripped and "the stick went right through his cheek". lesson? | Never run with things in your mouth | Lollipops are safe if small | Hold the stick while running | Only pencils are dangerous
test: my brother says a kid stood too close at badminton and "lost an eye". what's the true bit | Keep a safe distance from anyone swinging a racket | Badminton rackets can't hurt, they're light as a feather | Shuttlecocks are the only danger | Stand close so they see you
test: kids say the boy who got nits from the team helmet is "a dirty kid". pass it on? | No, shared gear can spread lice to anyone; don't spread it | Yes, he's dirty | Yes, and keep away from him so you don't catch them | Yes, and tell the coach
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the danger of sledding head first? | It makes head and neck injuries more likely | It is the safest way to sled, since the rider can see ahead | It makes the sled slower | It is dangerous only on flat ground
exam: Identify the principal reason for which sledding should cease when it becomes dark. | Obstacles can no longer be seen | The snow melts at night | Sleds cannot slide in the cold | Hills are closed at night
exam: It is given that a soccer goal wobbles. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | An adult shall be told, since unanchored goals can fall | The goal shall be climbed upon by several persons, so as to steady it | The goal shall be swung upon to test it | Nothing shall be done
exam: State the meaning of «football» in the United States and Canada. | American football | Soccer | Rugby union | Australian rules
